  "body": "**Scoped obstruction with an exact certificate. No new \u03ba, and the selected record is unchanged.**\n\nReordering commuting gates within segments, combined with any legal frame assignment, cannot lift the selected k8 word's packing ceiling by 5%, or even by 0.7%.\n\n- Track: complex supplier, seed 26.\n- Card: `complex-rank-shape-decoder`, with the scope **refined**.\n- Base: `0fdd7d82`.\n- Package: `research/k8-commutation-ceiling-seed26/`.\n\n## Why this is needed\n\nTwo merged scoped results bound the selected gauge44/k8 complex word (`c2b29430`):\n- **#1671** (bitplane) frees every frame but keeps the chronology fixed.\n- **seed27** (woklerx) frees commuting-ADD order but keeps frame slots fixed.\n\nNeither covers both at once, and #1671 lists that combination as open question 1. The rank-shape card asks for a decoder whose ceiling clears 5%, and this joint class is the natural place a cheap gain could hide.\n\n## Result\n\nAt a* = (21/20)\u00b7a_hi, the exact lower bound on **every** member's bill is **1 + 2.146289027469049 \u00d7 10\u207b\u2074 > 1**. A second certificate gives 1 + 4.86 \u00d7 10\u207b\u2076 > 1 at 1.007\u00b7a_hi.\n\n| Bound at a* | Value | Verdict |\n|---|---|---|\n| The selected word itself | 1.000245413040 | (member) |\n| Stage 1, chronological intervals | 1.000227868002 | rejects (#1671's case) |\n| Stage 1, commutation intervals (decoupled) | 0.999971231189 | does not reject |\n| **Stage 2, Lagrangian coupling** | **1 + 2.146 \u00d7 10\u207b\u2074** | **class excluded at 5%** |\n| Stage 2 at 1.007\u00b7a_hi | 1 + 4.86 \u00d7 10\u207b\u2076 | excluded at 0.7% |\n\nThe decoupled per-register bound leaves room once the chronology is relaxed. Coupling the two operands of each ADD gate is what closes it.\n\n## How\n\n1. **Commutation.**\n   - The rule: two ADDs t\u2190t+cs and t\u2032\u2190t\u2032+c\u2032s\u2032 commute when t \u2260 s\u2032 and t\u2032 \u2260 s; two SCALEs commute; any other pair commutes when it shares no register.\n   - It is checked against exact rational composition, in both orders, on all **109,388** same-register pairs.\n   - Of the 9,092 adjacent within-segment pairs, 4,519 commute.\n2. **Intervals.** A dependency DAG gives every gate an interval [lo2, hi2] that contains its frame in every member under every legal assignment.\n   - Two implementations agree: propagation, and a closed form over ancestor and descendant closures.\n   - In chronological mode both reduce to #1671's intervals exactly.\n   - Forced gates drop from 3,672 to 2,979.\n3. **Stage 1.** For each register, the cheapest chain of subspaces from its start to its end that passes through every item's interval, ignoring the order of items within the register. Each register's paid climbs in any member are at least this.\n4. **Stage 2.**\n   - Coupling: each free ADD gate's two copies get dimension-linear multipliers, +\u03bb on the target copy (charged at entry) and \u2212\u03bb on the source copy (charged at exit).\n   - Why it stays a lower bound: operand agreement cancels the multipliers in any real member, so the sum of the per-register optima remains a valid lower bound for every \u03bb.\n   - Exact arithmetic: costs use `ceiling.child_cost` lower ends on the 2\u207b\u00b2\u2075\u2076 grid, and \u03bb = k/2\u2074\u2070. The bound is therefore computed in exact integers.\n   - The certificates store the integers k: 1,008 in `LAMBDA.json` and 962 in `LAMBDA-1007.json`.\n   - `discover.py` regenerates such certificates; the verifier never imports it.\n\n`PROOF.md` gives the validity argument.\n\n## Controls\n\n- **Commutation rule:** exact on every pair, and a wrong rule (one that ignores t = s\u2032) is caught.\n- **Intervals:** the chronological intervals equal #1671's; the two trace implementations agree; the word's frames lie inside.\n- **Subspace enumerators:** two enumerators agree on every distinct interval, with counts 1, 2, 5, 16, 67, 374 and 2,825 for F\u2082^0 \u2026 F\u2082^6.\n- **The given word:** it reproduces #1671's profile and ceiling bracket, and the Stage-2 bound stays below its own bill, as it must.\n- **Toy exhaustive check (`toy.py`):** 150 random small words, 93 with several orders, checked over every linear extension and every frame assignment.\n  - The intervals contain every legal assignment.\n  - The Lagrangian bound never exceeds the exact minimum.\n  - Charging negative multipliers at the entry point (an invalid rule) is caught.\n- **Certificate files:** a multiplier on a forced gate is refused, and so is a certificate for a different word.\n\n## Checks run\n\n- `verify.py`: `PASS_K8_COMMUTATION_CEILING`, in about 30 s, with RESULT.json reproduced byte for byte.\n- 13 tests pass.\n- `research_package.py --check-only` passes.\n- The registered python3.12 replay: `PASS_REGISTERED_RESEARCH_PACKAGE`.\n- Run on Python 3.12.15 and 3.14.8 (macOS 26.5, arm64).\n\n## Scope\n\n**Covered.**\n- Every within-segment linear extension of the A, B and tail dependency orders, with coefficients unchanged.\n- Every frame assignment under #1671's contract.\n- Every exponent at or above the certified one.\n\n**Not covered.**\n- Cross-segment moves. 217 of the 485 adjacent pairs that straddle a segment boundary commute.\n- Coefficient-rescaling rewrites.\n- Changes to the cut, compensation, pins or endpoints.\n- New child interfaces: fanout (open #1865) and C3 gathers (Discussion 2032).\n- Open #1186's different cost model.\n- Other words.\n- A lift below 0.7%, which is neither shown nor excluded.\n\n**No \u03ba.**\n\n## Reproduce\n\n```sh\npython3 -I -B research/k8-commutation-ceiling-seed26/verify.py        # about 30 s; PASS_K8_COMMUTATION_CEILING\npython3 -I -B -m unittest discover -s research/k8-commutation-ceiling-seed26 -p test_package.py\npython3.12 scripts/research_package.py --package research/k8-commutation-ceiling-seed26\n```\n\n## Credit\n\n- **#1671** (bitplane, Gareth Davidson): its model, code and word are imported with digests pinned.\n- **seed27** (woklerx).\n- **#1817** (JosieElliston).\n- **navinor** (Aaron Wang): the frame method and `frame_model.py`.\n- **`complex-local-word-ceiling`:** `ceiling.py`.\n- **Related open work:** #1186 (mdumitrean), #1865 (1337isnot1337) and Discussion 2032.\n\n## Disclosure\n\nPrepared by Sagar Thakrar (thakrarsagar) with substantial AI assistance from Claude (Anthropic) via Claude Code.\n- Parallel read-only scout and judge subagents selected the question; both judges ranked it first.\n- Reader subagents and a synthesizer specified the model and found that operand coupling decides it.\n- The package code, certificates and controls were then written and checked.\n\nNo claim was posted to Discussions. New material is Apache-2.0.\n",
